# The document "How do your document preparation systems fare?": the numbers
# of the stress test of the Reference Manual, a few pages of the features
# that a long document needs, and the questions to put to any system.  The
# numbers are the ones measured by docs/BIG-DOCUMENTS.txt (section 7) and
# {c:docs/CHALLENGE-CLAIMS.txt} says where every statement comes from.  No
# statement is made about any other program.
.doc Strata: How do your document preparation systems fare?

.h1 How do your document preparation systems fare? [ch-challenge]
.center
A challenge, with the numbers of Strata
.center
Version @VERSION@
.center
Logic Magicians Software
.center
{latex:\lmslogo[0.8]}

Strata is a document preparation system for long technical documents.  Its
Reference Manual is one Strata document of 724 pages, and the Printer of Strata
prints it.  This document gives what was measured on that document, the
commands that give the numbers again, and a challenge: take the longest
document that you have, ask your system the same questions ({n:ch-questions}),
and send the answers.

Only what was measured on Strata is stated here.  Nothing is said about any
other program, because no other program was measured.

.h1 The document that was measured [ch-document]
The document is the Reference Manual of this release.  It has chapters,
numbered sections to four levels, a contents, an index and a list of figures,
and tables, pictures, equations and cross-references throughout.
.table 40 20
What | Measured
Pages of the PDF | 724
Size of the document on disk | 1910286 bytes
Items of the document (blocks, headlines and markers) | 11266
Lines of the document when laid out for the screen | 36795
Headlines | 1121
Index entries | 3251
Figures in the list of figures | 191
.end
{Fig:ch-open} shows the Editor with the whole document open.
.pic pictures/ch-open.png 5 The Editor with the whole manual open. [ch-open]

.h1 The machine [ch-machine]
Every number below was measured on one virtual machine of a farm, started
clean for the purpose: four virtual
processors of an Intel Xeon E5-2630L v4 at 1.8 GHz, 7.9 GB of memory, Ubuntu
26.04, pdfTeX 1.40.28 (TeX Live 2025).  The times are wall clock times.  A
faster machine gives smaller times; a number varies from one run to the next
by some tens of milliseconds.

.pagebreak
.h1 The Editor [ch-editor]
The first table gives the commands that were timed without a terminal (the
test program {c:tests/bigtest.c} runs the commands of a person on the
document in memory), the second the keys that were typed into the Editor in a
terminal ({c:tests/bigtui.py} types them into a pseudo-terminal and times each
one until the screen is quiet).
.table 48 12
Command, without a terminal | Time
Load the document | 87 ms
First layout of the 724 pages | 423 ms
Page Down from the start through the whole document | 60 ms
Search from the start to a word near the end | 326 ms
Search for a word that is not there | 14 ms
Replace all: 20393 occurrences of one word | 20 ms
The layout that follows the replacement | 295 ms
Go To the last section | 1 ms
Outline: collapse the whole document | 82 ms
Outline: show the headlines to level 3 | 59 ms
Type 40 characters, laying out after each one | 177 ms
Save | 131 ms
Load the saved document again | 23 ms
Peak memory of the whole run | 29.5 MB
.end
.table 48 12
Key, in a terminal | Time
First screen after the start | 552 ms
Page Down, mean of 30 | 6 ms
Page Down, the slowest of 30 | 40 ms
Ctrl-End and Ctrl-Home | 2 ms
Search from the start to the end | 492 ms
Go To section 3.5 | 5 ms
Outline to level 1 | 53 ms
A typed character, the slowest of 19 | 106 ms
Resident memory at the start | 23 MB
Resident memory at the end | 52 MB
.end
{Fig:ch-outline} shows the outline of the manual.
.pic pictures/ch-outline.png 5 The outline, to level 1. [ch-outline]

.h1 The Printer [ch-printer]
The Printer uses LaTeX for a beautiful layout.
.table 48 12
Print | Time
The whole manual as a PDF of 724 pages | 65 s
The same as a DVI file for the Viewer | 83 s
Sections 3 and 4 (187 pages) | 27 s
Sections 5 and 6 (146 pages) | 7 s
All the documents and PDF files of the manual set | 85 s
A synthetic document of 1312 pages as a PDF | 43 s
.end
The largest process of the Printer was 82 MB for the manual and 35 MB for the
synthetic document.  The rate of the whole manual is
{eq:\frac{{724}}{{65.2}}\approx 11.1}
pages every second.

.h1 What a long document needs besides speed [ch-features]
A document of hundreds of pages needs more than a fast editor.  These are the
things that the manual itself uses, and how they look in Strata.
.table 24 36
Feature | What Strata does
Equations | Written as TeX math, and drawn on the screen as typeset
 ({fig:ch-equation}).
Cross-references | Live cross references to a section, a figure or a page,
 kept up to date as the document is changed.
Contents, index, list of figures | Made automatically, and always right
 ({fig:ch-index}).
Structured sections | Numbered sections and subsections, an outline that
 collapses to a level, and Go To a section.
Tables | Rows and columns with borders ({fig:ch-table}).
Keep blocks | A region of blocks that stays on one page, so that a table or a
 figure with its caption is not split.
Print Error markers | A print with faults ends with a list in the Error Log,
 and a marker at each fault in the document ({fig:ch-errors}).
Line numbering | The lines are numbered in the margin ({fig:ch-numbered}).
 A number gives every reader the same exact place to point to: a reviewer
 writes "page 12, line 7" in a manual, and a lawyer or a judge cites a line
 of a contract or a brief.
.end
.pic pictures/ch-equation.png 3.2 Equations, text and result. [ch-equation]
.pic pictures/ch-table.png 3.2 A page of the manual with tables. [ch-table]
.pic pictures/ch-errors.png 5 Print Error markers at the faults. [ch-errors]
.pic pictures/ch-numbered.png 3.2 A page with numbered lines. [ch-numbered]
.pic pictures/ch-index.png 3.2 A page of the index of the manual. [ch-index]

.h1 The questions [ch-questions]
Take the longest document that you have, of at least several hundred pages, and
put these questions to the system that you use.  For Strata the number that
answers each one is in the tables of {n:ch-editor} and {n:ch-printer}.

1. How long does the document take to open, and how long to show the first
   screen?
2. How long does a key to the next page take, in the middle of the document?
3. How long does a search take from the first page to a word on the last page?
4. How long does it take to replace a common word in the whole document, and
   to lay the document out again?
5. How long does one typed character take in the middle of the document?
6. How long does the Save take?
7. How much memory is in use at the end?
8. How long does the whole document take to print to a PDF, and are the
   contents, the index and every cross-reference right in that PDF?
9. How long does a range of sections take to print?
10.  How long is it before the first page of a preview is shown?

How do your document preparation systems fare?

.h1 How to repeat the measurements [ch-repeat]
The commands are in the source tree of Strata.  The machine, the programs and
the versions are named in {n:ch-machine}; a different machine gives different
times.
.code
source scripts/setup -b release /tmp/strata-rel
scripts/strata-build term x11
scripts/strata-build manual
scripts/strata-build big
.end
The target manual makes the documents and the PDF files of the manual set, with
the Reference Manual as one document, and the target big runs the tests on a
synthetic document of more than a thousand pages: the Editor, the Printer, the
contents and the index checked against the pages, and the Viewer when it has
been built (the help of the script lists the targets).  The file
{c:docs/BIG-DOCUMENTS.txt} lists the commands that were used for the tables, one
by one, and the file {c:docs/CHALLENGE-CLAIMS.txt} says where every number and
every statement of this document comes from.
